WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.770
(pleasant music)

00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:06.073
- The artifacts speak louder than words,

00:00:06.073 --> 00:00:07.258
the way that we've preserved them.

00:00:07.258 --> 00:00:09.135
It's not what we said, it's what we did,

00:00:09.135 --> 00:00:10.927
and they all speak very loudly

00:00:10.927 --> 00:00:14.260
for generations and generations to come.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:20.056
My name is Barry Clifford,
and I'm an explorer.

00:00:22.104 --> 00:00:23.818
- [Narrator] Barry Clifford
has dedicated his life

00:00:23.818 --> 00:00:26.489
to finding and preserving
shipwreck treasure.

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:29.034
- Head towards the east, Chris.

00:00:29.034 --> 00:00:30.913
- [Chris] All right, stand by.

00:00:30.913 --> 00:00:32.619
- [Narrator] His team is
responsible for discovering

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:33.485
the Whydah,

00:00:33.485 --> 00:00:35.828
one of the largest pirate
shipwrecks in the world

00:00:35.828 --> 00:00:39.501
valued at over 400 million dollars.

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:42.712
- The Whydah was a slave
ship captured by pirates

00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:45.633
and it wrecked off of
Wellfleet, Massachusetts

00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.050
in April of 1717.

00:00:48.266 --> 00:00:50.337
I've been diving on it since 1982.

00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:52.455
I bought a boat and spent
the next couple of years

00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:53.765
looking for the ship.

00:00:53.765 --> 00:00:56.760
And then in 1984, we
found the first artifacts.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.727
And then we found the ship's bell.

00:00:58.727 --> 00:01:02.894
And the ship's bell said,
"The Whydah Gally, 1716."

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:08.774
Everything is buried at least
15 to 30 feet under the sand.

00:01:08.774 --> 00:01:11.774
So we have to dig a hole
sometimes 30 feet deep

00:01:11.774 --> 00:01:13.617
to get down to artifact layer.

00:01:13.617 --> 00:01:16.515
You know, you need precise
positioning equipment,

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:18.619
precise anchoring techniques,

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:20.881
and a way to dig very gently

00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:23.501
so you don't destroy or
disturb the artifacts.

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:26.171
There's some big chunks
of gold here, Chris.

00:01:26.171 --> 00:01:27.173
- [Chris] Wow, look at all the gold.

00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:28.251
Oh, my God.

00:01:28.251 --> 00:01:31.697
- I had no idea how much
stuff we would find.

00:01:31.697 --> 00:01:33.065
When you're talking about

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:35.204
hundreds of thousands of artifacts,

00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:37.566
you know, we have years
and years and years of work

00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:40.606
to conserve everything
that we've brought up.

00:01:40.606 --> 00:01:43.813
That's a spelt that might
have been used from a rum keg.

00:01:43.813 --> 00:01:45.526
- [Narrator] Unlike your
typical treasure hunter,

00:01:45.526 --> 00:01:48.259
Barry has never sold any of
the artifacts he's found,

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:52.240
choosing to put them on
display for the public to see.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.252
- I made a promise to myself early on

00:01:55.252 --> 00:01:57.004
that I was never gonna sell the treasure,

00:01:57.004 --> 00:01:58.703
that we would keep it all together.

00:01:58.703 --> 00:02:00.556
And my crew and my family

00:02:00.556 --> 00:02:03.040
and people who've worked
with me over the years

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.711
have all felt the same
way about preserving this

00:02:05.711 --> 00:02:07.247
for our cultural heritage.

00:02:07.247 --> 00:02:09.008
Take your time, no rush.

00:02:09.008 --> 00:02:11.187
I'd be lying if I said it wasn't fun.

00:02:11.187 --> 00:02:13.298
You're not gonna believe
what that is, Chris.

00:02:13.298 --> 00:02:17.010
I still get just as excited
about finding something

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.928
as I did 35 years ago.

00:02:18.928 --> 00:02:21.678
(pleasant music)

